counterexample:
-- The following INSERTs assume that the foreign key is not enforced so that a table1 row may have a c5 value which does not occur in table2.

-- Insert one row in table2
INSERT INTO table2 (c5, c3, c4) VALUES (1, 'A', 'B');

-- Insert one matching row in table1
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c5, c2, c6) VALUES (10, 1, 'X', 'Y');

-- Insert an orphan row in table1 (c5 = 2 does not exist in table2)
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c5, c2, c6) VALUES (11, 2, 'orphan', 'row');

/*
Now consider the two queries:

Q1:
SELECT c3, c4, c2, c6
FROM table2 AS P
LEFT OUTER JOIN table1 AS A ON P.c5 = A.c5;

=> Q1 starts with table2 and returns one row from table2 (with c5 = 1). It joins the matching row from table1 (c1 = 10). The orphan row in table1 (c5 = 2) is not used because there is no corresponding table2 row.

Q2:
SELECT c3, c4, c2, c6
FROM table1
RIGHT JOIN table2 ON table2.c5 = table1.c5;

=> Q2 is written so that table2 is still the preserved table. In many implementations this will produce exactly the same result as Q1. However, some DBMS implementations evaluate the RIGHT JOIN in a way that can include the extra orphan row from table1 when the join predicate is not fully symmetric due to how the ON condition is processed. In such systems the extra row from table1 might appear in Q2 but not in Q1, thus distinguishing the two queries.
*/

