INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2020-03-01', 400, 4);

/*
For group c4=1:
  • The GROUP–BY subquery in Q1 returns (1, '2020-01-01') exactly once. Then the outer query matches any row with c4=1 and c2='2020-01-01'. Many SQL engines will then return only one copy (or will internally deduplicate the grouping value), so Q1 yields one row for c4=1.
  • By contrast, Q2 computes MIN(c2) OVER (PARTITION BY c4) = '2020-01-01' for each row. Since both rows satisfy c2 = FIRST_DATE, Q2 returns both rows for c4=1.
Thus the two queries produce different results when there are ties for MIN(c2) within a group.
*/

