/*
Under this counterexample the numerator in Q1 becomes:
  group 1: 2 (because two rows have 2020-01-02, which equals 2020-01-01+1 day)
  group 2: 1
Sum = 3 and the denominator (number of groups) = 2 so Q1 returns ROUND(3/2,2)=1.50.

By contrast, Q2 counts distinct groups that qualify. Both groups qualify (each has at least one row with date = minimum date + 1), so it returns ROUND(2/2,2)=1.00.

Thus the two queries yield different results.

NOTE: In a properly normalized table that obeys the PRIMARY KEY (c4, c2) constraint the above situation cannot occur. This counterexample is meant to illustrate how the two queries differ semantically if duplicates (or extra rows) occur in the data source used for reporting.
*/

