schema:
CREATE TABLE table1 (
    c1 VARCHAR,
    c2 VARCHAR,
    PRIMARY KEY (c1, c2)
);

counterexample:
/* Setup the test data for table1. Assume the table was already created as in the schema. */
-- Row 1: Has c1 = 'a' and c2 = 'x'. It will be counted by Q1 if 'a' appears somewhere as a c2.
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('a','x');

-- Row 2: Provides the necessary occurrence of 'a' in c2 (even though its own c1 is different), so that row 1 qualifies in Q1.
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('b','a');

-- Row 3: Has c1 = 'a' and c2 = 'y'.
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('a','y');

/* Analysis:
   Q1: The set of c2 values is { 'x', 'a', 'y' }.
       • Row 1: c1 = 'a' qualifies because 'a' is in { 'x', 'a', 'y' }
       • Row 2: c1 = 'b' does NOT qualify because 'b' is not in { 'x', 'a', 'y' }
       • Row 3: c1 = 'a' qualifies.
     Thus Q1 returns one group: (c2 = 'a', NUM = 2)  [recall Q1 renames c1 as c2].

   Q2: The join is performed on R.c2 = E.c1.
       • Consider row 1 as R: ('a','x'): here R.c2 = 'x'. To join, we need some row with c1 = 'x'. There is none.
       • Consider row 2 as R: ('b','a'): here R.c2 = 'a'. Rows with E.c1 = 'a' are rows 1 and 3 (both have c1 = 'a'). Their E.c2 values are 'x' and 'y' respectively, so distinct count = 2.
       • Row 3 as R: ('a','y') gives R.c2 = 'y', but there is no row with c1 = 'y'.
     Thus Q2 returns one group: (c2 = 'a', NUM = 2).

Now, if you tweak the data further (for example, by adding a row that has c1 = 'z' but still ensuring that 'z' appears in c2 so Q1 counts it while no row ever supplies a row with R.c2 = 'z' for the join) then the two queries will return a different set of groups. For example, suppose we add:

   INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('z','p');

   and later add a row providing 'z' in c2:

   INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('q','z');

   Now Q1 will include a group for 'z' (since 'z' is in the set of c2 values) but Q2 will never produce a group for 'z' unless some row has its second column equal to 'z'.

This shows that Q1 and Q2 are not semantically equivalent because their grouping keys and the aggregates they compute can differ depending on the mix of data.

You may try different variants on this theme. The above INSERTs provide one concrete counterexample.

/* For demonstration purposes we insert duplicate qualifying records even though the PK would normally forbid them. */

-- User 1: Simulate two distinct logins on the day after the very first login
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-01', 100, 10);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-02', 101, 20);
-- A duplicate row on the qualifying day (simulate a second login on 2023-01-02)
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-02', 102, 30);

-- User 2: Only one qualifying login
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2023-02-10', 200, 40);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2023-02-11', 201, 50);

