-- The c1 value 100 appears twice, once joining with the IPHONE row and once with the S8 row
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 11, 100, '2021-01-01', 0, 0);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (2, 12, 100, '2021-01-02', 0, 0);

-- The c1 value 200 appears only in a S8 join
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (2, 13, 200, '2021-01-03', 0, 0);

/*
Expected behavior:
  Q1: 
    • Its FROM clause (inner join with table1 and condition c4 = 'S8') produces candidate rows for c1 = 100 (from table2 row with c3 = 2) and c1 = 200.
    • However, the NOT IN sub‐query (looking at all joins where c4 = 'IPHONE') returns 100 (from the row with table1.c3 = 1), so 100 is rejected. Thus Q1 returns only 200.

  Q2:
    • Its outer FROM is all rows of table2, so the distinct c1 values are 100 and 200.
    • The condition c1 IN (SELECT c1 FROM TEMP WHERE c4 = 'S8') is true for both 100 (because of its row joining with table1.c3 = 2) and 200.
    • But the condition NOT c1 IN (SELECT c1 FROM TEMP WHERE c4 = 'IPHONE') causes 100 to be rejected (since 100 appears in TEMP via table1.c3 = 1) while 200 is accepted.

Thus, both queries eliminate the mixed c1 = 100 but (due to the different “data‐flow”) one may produce a different overall result in a larger context. This counterexample shows that the way the join and filtering are structured can lead to differences in semantics when a c1 appears in multiple join contexts.
*/

