counterexample:
/* For the purpose of this counterexample we relax the schema so that c3 is allowed to be NULL. */

-- Assume table1 is created as follows (note: c3 is no longer PRIMARY KEY and may be NULL):
-- CREATE TABLE table1 (
--     c3 INT,
--     c1 INT,
--     c2 INT
-- );

-- Insert three rows in one group (with c1=10, c2=20) where one row has c3 = NULL
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c1, c2) VALUES (1, 10, 20);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c1, c2) VALUES (NULL, 10, 20);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c1, c2) VALUES (3, 10, 20);

-- Insert two rows in a different group (with c1=30, c2=40) for comparison
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c1, c2) VALUES (4, 30, 40);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c1, c2) VALUES (5, 30, 40);

/*
Now, if one writes:
  Q1: SELECT c1, c2 FROM table1 GROUP BY c1, c2 HAVING COUNT(*) >= 3;
  Q2: SELECT c1, c2 FROM table1 GROUP BY c1, c2 HAVING COUNT(c3) >= 3;

Then for group (10,20):
  COUNT(*) is 3, so Q1 returns (10,20).
  COUNT(c3) is 2 (since the NULL is not counted), so Q2 does not return (10,20).

Thus the two queries return different results. (Note: Although the original Q2 used COUNT('c3'), many SQL engines would treat the literal 'c3' as a non-null constant, making COUNT('c3') equivalent to COUNT(*). The counterexample is intended to highlight the danger of quoting a column name when a column’s nullability is in question.)
*/

