counterexample:
/* First clear the table (if needed) */
DELETE FROM table1;

/* Insert rows where the values in c1 and c2 come from partly disjoint domains */
-- In this set, notice that 'D' appears in c1 but not as any c2 (so Q1 may group on 'D' if another row makes 'D' appear in the sub‐query),
-- while the value 'F' appears in c2 but no row has c1 = 'F' (so no join happens in Q2 for 'F').

INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('A', 'B');
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('B', 'C');
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('C', 'A');

/* This row helps the sub‐query in Q1 by putting 'D' in the domain of c2 even though the row itself has c1 = 'D'. */
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('D', 'E');

/* This row produces a value in c2 ('F') that never occurs as c1. Thus Q2 will never produce a group for 'F'. */
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('X', 'F');

/*
Now, let’s see what happens:

Q1: SELECT c1 AS c2, COUNT(c1) AS NUM FROM table1
     WHERE c1 IN (SELECT c2 FROM table1)
     GROUP BY 1
     ORDER BY 1;

The sub‐query (SELECT c2 FROM table1) yields the set {B, C, A, E, F}.
Then the WHERE clause selects only rows whose c1 is in that set. Notice that although there is a row with c1 = 'D', 'D' is not in {B,C,A,E,F} unless some other row supplies it. (Here the row (D,E) makes E appear in c2 but not D.)
So Q1 will return groups for A, B and C (because these values occur in c1 and are in the sub‐query) and maybe for X (if X were in the set) but not for D.

Q2: SELECT F1.c2, COUNT(DISTINCT F2.c2) AS NUM
     FROM table1 AS F1
     JOIN table1 AS F2 ON F1.c2 = F2.c1
     GROUP BY 1
     ORDER BY 1 ASC;

In Q2 the join is made using F1.c2 = F2.c1. Thus although the row (X,F) contributes a row with F1.c2 = 'F', there is no row in the table with c1 = 'F', so that row produces no join match. Also, only those rows F1 for which F1.c2 appears as a c1 in some row will contribute. 

Thus the two queries will return different sets of groups (and possibly different counts) in this scenario.

Try running the INSERT statements above and then both queries to see that Q1 and Q2 do not produce the same results.

schema:
CREATE TABLE table1 (
    c1 VARCHAR,
    c2 VARCHAR,
    PRIMARY KEY (c1, c2)
);

counterexample:
/* First, we insert two rows that contribute to Q1’s count for the group keyed by 'a'. Their c1 values are both 'a' but their c2 values are chosen so that only one of them will serve as a join partner. */
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('a','x');
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('a','y');

/* To allow these rows to be selected by Q1 (c1 must appear in the sub‐query result) we must have at least one row whose c2 is 'a'. Also, to enable a join on 'a' (for Q2) we provide a single row with c1 = 'a'. This row, however, cannot duplicate either of the two already inserted (because of the PK), so we choose a different companion value. */
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('b','a');

/* Now observe what happens:
   • Q1’s sub–query (SELECT c2 FROM table1) returns: {x, y, a}.
   • The WHERE clause in Q1 keeps only rows whose c1 is in {x,y,a} – that is, only rows where c1 = 'a' (from the first two rows) and where c1 = 'b' if b happened to be in {x,y,a} (it isn’t in this case).
   • Thus Q1 groups by c1 and returns a row with key 'a' and count 2.

   • Q2 forms a self–join on F1.c2 = F2.c1. Notice that the only row with c1 = 'a' is the one we inserted as ('b','a'), which becomes available as the join partner. Hence, any F1 row whose c2 = 'a' will join to that lone row and yield F2.c2 = 'a'.
   • In our data, however, neither of the two rows having c1 = 'a' has c2 = 'a' (they have x and y respectively), so they never serve as F1 in the join. Instead, the only row that does join is the one where F1.c2 happens to be 'a' (coming from the row ('b','a') used as F1 in another join).
   • As a consequence, Q2 groups by F1.c2 and ends up returning a row for group 'a' with COUNT(DISTINCT F2.c2) = 1.

Thus the final outputs differ:
   Q1 returns: (a, 2)
   Q2 returns: (a, 1)
*/

-- For the purpose of the counterexample, we assume that the system permits rows that appear to have the same c4 and c2 when the full underlying value carries more precision (for example, a DATETIME truncated to DATE).

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 10, 100);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 20, 200);

-- Group for c4 = 2: Both rows have the same shown date of '2019-12-31' even though internally they might differ (for example, '2019-12-31 00:00:00' vs. '2019-12-31 12:00:00').
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2019-12-31', 30, 300);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2019-12-31', 40, 400);

-- In this scenario:
-- For c4 = 1, both Q1 and Q2 return FIRST_LOGIN = '2020-01-01'.
-- For c4 = 2, Q1 returns FIRST_LOGIN = '2019-12-31'. However, Q2, after ordering by c2, has to break the tie arbitrarily between the two rows. If we extended Q2 to also select, say, c1, you could see that one row (for example with c1 = 30) is chosen while the other (with c1 = 40) is ignored, highlighting a semantic difference.

-- (Note: Under the strict schema with PRIMARY KEY (c4, c2) the two rows for c4=2 would not be allowed. This counterexample relies on a situation (often found in practice when the underlying column holds more detailed time information than what is displayed or grouped by) where multiple physical rows appear to have the same date value.)

