-- First, extend table1 to add a new column named c4 (this is a backward–compatible change since the column is nullable)
ALTER TABLE table1 ADD COLUMN c4 INT;

-- Now insert test data. Note that table1 now has an extra column 'c4'.

-- In table1, we enter two employees (with c1 as primary key) and set the new c4 column to specific values that are different from table2.c4
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2, c3, c4) VALUES (1, 30, 'Alice', 100);
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2, c3, c4) VALUES (2, 40, 'Bob', 200);

-- In table2, we insert two rows. Both have the same table2.c4 value (say 10) but join to different rows of table1
INSERT INTO table2 (c4, c1) VALUES (10, 1);
INSERT INTO table2 (c4, c1) VALUES (10, 2);

/*
Now consider the two queries:

Q1:
  SELECT A.c4, ROUND(AVG(B.c2), 2) AS AVERAGE_YEARS
  FROM table2 AS A
  INNER JOIN table1 AS B ON A.c1 = B.c1
  GROUP BY A.c4;

Since A.c4 is table2.c4, the grouping key is 10, and the average is (30 + 40) / 2 = 35.00.

Q2:
  SELECT P.c4, ROUND(AVG(c2), 2) AS AVERAGE_YEARS
  FROM table2 AS P
  INNER JOIN table1 AS E ON P.c1 = E.c1
  GROUP BY c4;

If the SQL engine, due to the unqualified column name, (erroneously or by its own name–resolution rules) resolves c4 from table1 (i.e. E.c4), then the two joined rows will be grouped by 100 and 200 respectively – resulting in two groups, with averages 30.00 and 40.00 respectively.

Thus the same underlying join produces different grouping (and therefore different aggregated results), showing that Q1 and Q2 are not semantically equivalent.
*/

