*/

-- User 1: installs on 2021-01-01 and has two distinct sessions on the next day (2021-01-02)
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2021-01-01', 10, 100);
-- Simulate two different sessions on the same day by varying one of the other columns
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2021-01-02', 11, 101);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2021-01-02', 12, 102);

-- User 2: installs on 2021-01-01 and shows one session the next day (normal behavior)
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2021-01-01', 20, 200);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2021-01-02', 21, 201);

/*
Now, consider what each query returns for installation date 2021-01-01:

For Q1:
  – The subquery A1 finds two users (c4=1 and c4=2) with FIRST_LOGIN = '2021-01-01'.
  – When joined with table1, user 1 produces two rows on 2021-01-02 where (FIRST_LOGIN + 1) = '2021-01-02', and user 2 produces one such row.
  – Therefore, the numerator becomes 2 + 1 = 3 while the denominator remains 2; so DAY1_RETENTION = ROUND(3/2, 2) = 1.5 (or 150%).

For Q2:
  – In the CTE the window function orders sessions per user. For user 1, the ordered sessions are: '2021-01-01' (rk=1, next_login = '2021-01-02') and one of the 2021-01-02 rows gets rk=2. Thus only the first session is used, counting a single retention event. For user 2 the behavior is similar (one retention event).
  – The aggregated retention becomes (1 + 1)/2 = 1 (or 100%).

Thus the two queries return different DAY1_RETENTION values for install date 2021-01-01.
*/

