-- Insert data for TABLE1
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c3, c2) VALUES (1, 'Alice', 30);
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c3, c2) VALUES (2, 'Bob', 40);

-- Insert data for TABLE2
-- Employee 1 appears with two different c4 values, representing two different projects/departments
INSERT INTO table2 (c4, c1) VALUES (100, 1);
INSERT INTO table2 (c4, c1) VALUES (200, 1);

-- Employee 2 appears with only one project
INSERT INTO table2 (c4, c1) VALUES (200, 2);

/*
Now consider the two queries:

Q1:
  SELECT A.c4, ROUND(AVG(B.c2), 2) AS AVERAGE_YEARS
  FROM table2 AS A
  INNER JOIN table1 AS B ON A.c1 = B.c1
  GROUP BY A.c4;

Q2:
  SELECT P.c4, ROUND(AVG(E.c2), 2) AS AVERAGE_YEARS
  FROM table2 AS P
  INNER JOIN table1 AS E ON P.c1 = E.c1
  GROUP BY P.c4;

For project (or department) 100, only Alice appears so the average is 30.
For project 200, both Alice and Bob appear. However, note that Alice contributes her value 30 via two different rows (once from her own join from project 200 and once from her duplicate row if the data were interpreted differently in the application logic), whereas Bob contributes once his value 40. Depending on the intended interpretation – averaging per actual employee versus per join occurrence – one formulation in a larger application might inadvertently “double‐count" the employee’s contribution. 

Thus, although the SQL as written is syntactically similar, if one intended Q2 to avoid the double–counting effect (by, for example, deduplicating the table1 values before averaging) while Q1 does not, then the two queries will produce different answers. This counterexample illustrates how the same underlying data can lead to different aggregated results if the join multiplicity is not taken into account.
*/

