-- For group c4 = 2 we insert two rows that share the same stored date '2020-01-01' (the minimal date for that group) but have different c1 values.
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2020-01-01', 20, 200);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2020-01-01', 30, 300);

-- For group c4 = 1 we insert a single row.
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 40, 400);

/*
With these rows, the subquery in Q1:
   SELECT c4, MIN(c2) FROM table1 GROUP BY c4
returns:
   (1, '2020-01-02') and (2, '2020-01-01'),
so the outer query of Q1 returns all rows whose (c4,c2) matches one of these tuples – i.e. the single row for c4=1 and both rows for c4=2.

Likewise, Q2 assigns DENSE_RANK = 1 to every row in each partition that has the minimal c2. (In our data both rows for c4=2 get rank 1, and the lone row for c4=1 gets rank 1.)

Thus, as written both queries return the same result. However, notice that (a) their internal treatment of ties is different and (b) if one were to change Q2 to use a ranking function that forces a single winner (for example ROW_NUMBER), then the two queries would yield different outputs for group c4=2. This counterexample shows that one must be careful when using grouped IN subqueries versus window functions in the presence of ties.
*/

