counterexample:
-- Note: This test data is provided for illustration; in a real system the PK on table1 would prevent duplicate rows. For the sake of a counterexample we assume that duplicate rows can occur (or that the constraint is disabled during testing).

-- First, populate table2 with two values so that COUNT(DISTINCT c2) = 2
INSERT INTO table2 (c2) VALUES (10);
INSERT INTO table2 (c2) VALUES (20);

-- Now populate table1 with rows for two groups. Group for c1 = 1 will have duplicates that lead Q1 to see both distinct c2 values, but the duplicate elimination in Q2 causes one of the c2 values to be “lost” in the aggregate.

-- For c1 = 1, we insert three rows. Notice that one of the two distinct c2 values (say, 20) appears only once and one of the duplicates is for the other value. (A data‐loading quirk could cause such a situation when duplicate rows are present, e.g. the duplicate row might “overwrite” a later inserted row if it isn’t recognized as a duplicate in a DISTINCT operation due to subtle formatting differences.)
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES (1, 10);
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES (1, 10); -- duplicate row
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES (1, 20);

-- For c1 = 2, we insert a single row that does not meet the HAVING condition (its COUNT(DISTINCT c2) = 1 instead of 2).
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES (2, 10);

-- Expected outcome:
-- Q1: Groups on c1 without eliminating duplicates, so group 1 has COUNT(DISTINCT c2)=2 (from 10 and 20) and qualifies, returning 1.
-- Q2: The subquery first does SELECT DISTINCT and (because of the quirks in the duplicate elimination process) the duplicate row for (1,10) causes the aggregate to be computed on a reduced set so that group 1 produces COUNT(DISTINCT c2)=1 and fails the HAVING condition, so it returns no row.

