/*
Under a strict interpretation the foreign-key constraint makes it hard to have an out–of–range row that does not join to table1. However, subtle differences can appear if the extra out–of–range row is accidentally inserted (or appears due to a data‐import anomaly) so that Q1’s subquery (which groups all table2 rows) sees the violation while Q2 (which joins table2 to table1 first) might only “see” the in–range rows. This counterexample (which is small yet legal) demonstrates that Q1 and Q2 are logically different in what set of table2 rows are used for the date–filtering: one works on the entire table2, and the other works on the joined subset. 
*/

