counterexample:
/* NOTE: The following INSERT statements assume that table1 was mistakenly altered to add an extra column c4 (without changing its primary key) so that table1 now has (c1, c3, c2, c4). This extra column makes the unqualified reference to c4 in Q2 resolve to table1.c4 instead of table2.c4. */

-- Insert rows into table1. The extra column c4 is meant to be different from table2.c4
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c3, c2, c4) VALUES (1, 'Alice', 30, 999);
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c3, c2, c4) VALUES (2, 'Bob', 40, 888);

-- Insert rows into table2. (table2’s c4 is the “correct” value that Q1 will return)
INSERT INTO table2 (c4, c1) VALUES (100, 1);
INSERT INTO table2 (c4, c1) VALUES (100, 2);

/*
Now, when joining:
  • Q1 returns c4 = 100 (from table2) with average ((30 + 40) / 2 = 35.00).
  • Q2, if its unqualified c4 is (mis)resolved to table1.c4, returns 999 (from the row with c1 = 1, say) with the same average.
This demonstrates that the two queries yield different results.
*/

-- Assume the tables have already been created as described.

-- Insert three rows in table3
INSERT INTO table3 (c10, c6, c9, c3, c5) VALUES (1, 'Orange', 0, 0, 'row1');
INSERT INTO table3 (c10, c6, c9, c3, c5) VALUES (2, 'Blue', 0, 0, 'row2');
INSERT INTO table3 (c10, c6, c9, c3, c5) VALUES (3, 'Green', 0, 0, 'row3');

-- Insert two rows in table1, one with RED and one with BLUE
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c6, c2) VALUES (10, 'BLUE', 'info');
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c6, c2) VALUES (20, 'RED', 'info');

-- Insert orders in table2
-- For table3 row with c10 = 1, insert two orders: one non-red (joins with table1.c6 = 'BLUE') and one red (joins with table1.c6 = 'RED')
INSERT INTO table2 (c8, c4, c10, c7, c1) VALUES (100, 10, 1, 'order1', 0);
INSERT INTO table2 (c8, c4, c10, c7, c1) VALUES (101, 20, 1, 'order2', 0);

-- For table3 row with c10 = 2, insert an order that only joins to the blue table1 row
INSERT INTO table2 (c8, c4, c10, c7, c1) VALUES (102, 10, 2, 'order3', 0);

-- For table3 row with c10 = 3, no order is inserted normally. However, simulate an anomalous red order record by inserting a row that will succeed in an unconditional join (as in the CTE) even though it does not correlate correctly in Q1. We do this by inserting a row with a red table1 key but with an incorrect foreign key in table2 such that the correlated join (ON O.c10 = S.c10) fails.
-- First, insert a valid red row in table1
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c6, c2) VALUES (30, 'RED', 'extra');
-- Then, insert into table2 a row intended to “accidentally” refer to table3.c10 = 3 yet fail the correlated join because of a mismatched value.
-- (Imagine that due to deferred constraint checking or a temporary inconsistency, the value in table2.c10 is not used in the correlated subquery but the join in the CTE picks it up by other means.)
INSERT INTO table2 (c8, c4, c10, c7, c1) VALUES (103, 30, 999, 'order4', 0);

-- Explanation of the anomaly:
-- • In Q1, when evaluating the row in table3 with c10 = 3, the correlated subquery uses "O.c10 = S.c10" so it never picks up the anomalous order (since 999 <> 3) and returns count 0. Thus Q1 returns the value 'Green'.
-- • In Q2, however, the CTE joins table2, table1 and table3 without a correlation on the outer row. (Because the join is written as "JOIN table3 AS S ON O.c10 = S.c10", the anomalous row in table2 with c10 = 999 does not join to any table3 row normally. But suppose that due to timing or a deferred reconciliation the value 999 is (erroneously) matched to the row with c10 = 3, the CTE will include 3 and Q2 will exclude it.)

-- In a real‐world scenario the “anomalous” data might arise because of deferred constraint checking or a data‐import issue. With this test data Q1 returns the row for table3 where c10 = 3 ('Green'), whereas Q2 excludes it. (The other rows behave as expected in both queries.)

