/*
Expected behavior:
  • Q1 will join table2 and table1 to produce three rows for c4 = 100 with table1.c2 values 10, 20, and 10, so the AVG is (10 + 20 + 10) / 3 = 13.33.
  • Q2, after grouping by c4, will return one row per distinct group. However, the DISTINCT in the SELECT clause will remove duplicate aggregate rows if they occur. In some SQL implementations the duplicate join row may be eliminated before computing the aggregate so that only one instance of c1=1 is considered; then the average would be (10 + 20) / 2 = 15.00.
Thus the two queries would return different AVERAGE_YEARS for c4 = 100.

NOTE: Under strict adherence to the schema constraints the duplicate row in table2 is not allowed. This counterexample is meant to illustrate that, if duplicate join rows were to occur, Q1 and Q2 would not be semantically equivalent.
*/

