INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES (1, 1);
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES (1, 1);
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES (2, 2);

/*
Now, consider what the queries return:

For Q1:
  For each group in table1, COUNT(DISTINCT c2) is computed.
    • Group with c1 = 1: COUNT(DISTINCT c2) = 1
    • Group with c1 = 2: COUNT(DISTINCT c2) = 1
  The subquery (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT c2) FROM table2) yields 1 since the duplicate is collapsed.
  Thus both groups satisfy 1 = 1 and appear in Q1’s result.

For Q2:
  For each group in table1, COUNT(DISTINCT c2) is still 1. However, the subquery (SELECT COUNT(c2) FROM table2) now returns 2 (because both rows—even though duplicates—are counted).
  Therefore 1 ≠ 2 and no group qualifies under Q2.

This test data shows that Q1 returns rows (c1=1 and c1=2) while Q2 returns no rows, so the two queries are not equivalent.
*/

