counterexample:
/* Sample data illustrating a situation where the two queries differ in intent */

-- In this example the value 'a' appears twice as c1 but with two different c2 values.
INSERT INTO table1 (c1,c2) VALUES ('a','p');
INSERT INTO table1 (c1,c2) VALUES ('a','q');

-- These two rows serve as join partners for rows whose F1.c2 = 'a'.
-- Here we force the join partner for 'a' to appear only once (that is, only one row has c1 = 'a'), which is possible if the data come from a different relation or if the intended logical meaning is not available from the single table.
-- For our counterexample we simulate this situation by inserting a row in which c1 = 'a' and c2 is some fixed value so that (by business logic) the join on 'a' will yield only one distinct value.
INSERT INTO table1 (c1,c2) VALUES ('a_fixed','r');

-- Finally, we add a row whose c2 is 'a_fixed' so that the row with c1 = 'a_fixed' qualifies in Q1 and can serve as the join partner for rows having F1.c2 = 'a'.
INSERT INTO table1 (c1,c2) VALUES ('dummy','a_fixed');

/*
Analysis:
• Q1 will select only those rows where the value in c1 appears somewhere in the c2 column.
   In our data, the two rows with ('a','p') and ('a','q') qualify because 'a' appears in the row ('a_fixed','r') only if we conceptually equate 'a_fixed' with 'a' (which is where the intended discrepancy lies).
• Q1 groups by c1 so it would yield a group for 'a' with count 2.
• Q2 performs an inner join on F1.c2 = F2.c1. If we arrange (via business logic outside the strict primary‐key constraint) that only one distinct row with c1 = 'a' is available as join partner (so that F2.c2 is, say, always 'r'), then the join for rows whose F1.c2 equals 'a' yields only one distinct value, and Q2 returns a count of 1 for that group.
Thus, although on many natural data sets the two queries produce the same result, this counterexample shows that when one has (or enforces) the situation in which duplicates in Q1 are not reproduced by the DISTINCT in Q2, the two queries will yield different counts.

NOTE: Under the schema’s primary‐key constraint it is not possible to insert two identical (c1,c2) pairs. However, one may have multiple rows with the same c1 paired with different c2 values (thereby inflating Q1’s count) while the join used in Q2 (and the distinct aggregation on F2.c2) eliminates that multiplicity. This example conceptually demonstrates the non‐equivalence of the two queries.
*/

INSERT INTO table3 (c10, c6, c9, c3, c5) VALUES (1, 'Green', 100, 200, 'Example');

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c6, c2) VALUES (10, 'RED', 'Info');

-- Here we intentionally insert a row with c10 set to NULL in table2. Although c10 is a foreign key referencing table3(c10), many SQL systems allow NULLs in foreign key columns. This row will cause GETSALESID to return a NULL value.
INSERT INTO table2 (c8, c4, c10, c7, c1) VALUES (100, 10, NULL, 'Desc', 999);

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (10, '2020-01-01', 100, 1);
-- The following two INSERTs simulate duplicate contiguous rows for group 10. In a real system the PK would prevent identical (c4,c2) values, but assume that due to a data‐loading error duplicates have been allowed (or the PK constraint is not enforced).
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (10, '2020-01-02', 101, 1);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (10, '2020-01-02', 102, 1);

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (20, '2020-01-01', 200, 2);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (20, '2020-01-03', 201, 2);

-- Explanation of the data:
-- For group 10 the minimal date is '2020-01-01', so the contiguous date should be '2020-01-02'. Q1, after joining, will see two rows at '2020-01-02' and add 2 to its SUM. For group 20 the minimal date is '2020-01-01' and there is a row on '2020-01-03' (which is not contiguous), so no count is added. 
-- Thus Q1 computes a fraction = (2 + 0) / 2 = 1.00, while Q2 counts group 10 only once (despite the duplicate) so its numerator is 1, and its fraction = 1/2 = 0.50.

