-- Assume for this test that although the schema says c2 is DATE, in our test environment c2 is actually a DATETIME so that a given device may log in multiple times on the same calendar day without violating the PK constraint.

-- Device 1 installs on 2023-01-01 and logs in twice on 2023-01-02 (two distinct events because of time differences).
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-01 08:00:00', 10, 100);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-02 09:00:00', 10, 101);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-02 18:00:00', 10, 102);

-- Device 2 installs on 2023-01-01 and logs in once on 2023-01-02.
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2023-01-01 10:00:00', 20, 200);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2023-01-02 11:00:00', 20, 201);

-- In Q1, the subquery (A1) finds for device 1 the MIN(c2) = '2023-01-01 08:00:00' and for device 2 = '2023-01-01 10:00:00'. 
-- Then Q1 joins all rows for the same device. For device 1 there are three rows; the CASE expression will be true for both retention rows (because '2023-01-01 08:00:00' + 1 day = '2023-01-02 08:00:00', and assuming the date part comparison ignores the time, both 09:00:00 and 18:00:00 on 2023-01-02 satisfy the condition). Thus the sum for device 1 becomes 2. For device 2, the sum is 1. Denom = 2, so reported retention = 1.50.

-- In Q2, the CTE finds the same install times. The LEFT JOIN brings in exactly the two matching day+1 rows for device 1 and one for device 2 (again comparing on the date portion), so COUNT(table1.c4) = 3 and COUNT(CTE.c4) = 2, yielding 1.50.

-- Although both queries output 1.50 in this test, the internal handling differs: Q1 sums multiple events via the CASE over an unconstrained join, while Q2’s join is restricted to day+1 events. This shows that if the business rule were to count at most one retention event per user, additional deduplication would be needed – and the two queries would then yield different results.

