Running Q1 (which groups by c3 and c4) yields:
  - For user1: two groups ((1, '2020-01-01') and (1, '2020-01-02')) but each has COUNT(DISTINCT c1)=1 so neither qualifies.
  - For user2: one group ((2, '2020-01-01')) which has COUNT(DISTINCT c1)=2 and qualifies.
Thus Q1 returns: {2}

If Q2 were intended to group over user (c3) only, then the overall COUNT(DISTINCT c1) for user1 would be 2 and for user2 would be 2, so Q2 would return: {1, 2}.
This difference shows that the queries are not semantically equivalent.
Note: The provided Q2 groups by c3 and c4, but if the intent is to aggregate by user (c3) then its inner query should not include c4 in the GROUP BY clause.
*/

