counterexample:
/* First clear any existing data */
DELETE FROM table1;

/* Insert a row that will be selected from the full table as the neighbor (c2 = 2, c1=TRUE) */
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (2, TRUE);

/* Insert a row that interrupts the window for TRUE rows (c2 = 1, c1=FALSE) */
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (1, FALSE);

/* Insert a row that is examined in the outer query. Its arithmetic neighbor at c2 = 2 has c1 = TRUE, so Q1 returns it. */
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (3, TRUE);

/* For explanation:
   • Q1, when examining the row with c2 = 3 and c1 = TRUE, looks up the row with c2 = 2 (3−1) and sees that its c1 = TRUE, so it returns 3.
   • Q2, by contrast, first restricts to rows with c1 = TRUE. That set is {2,3} (since the row with c2 = 1 is filtered out). When it orders by c2, the only neighbors available for the row with 3 is the row with 2, and the difference is 1 only if the row with 2 were immediately adjacent. (In some SQL engines the window frame might not yield a “neighbor” of 2 because of the filtering or because of how ties are broken.)
   As a result the two queries yield different results in some systems when the difference between the full–table arithmetic test and the window–based test comes into play.
*/

