counterexample:
/* The INSERT statements below assume that duplicate rows with the same (c4, c2) are allowed (for example, if constraints are deferred or if table1 is a view over an underlying source with duplicates) */

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2001-01-01', 10, 100);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2001-01-01', 20, 200);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2001-02-01', 30, 300);

-- In the following inserts the same table3 key (c10 = 1) is referred to by two rows in table2. One of these joins via table1 to a red record and the other joins to a non‐red record.

-- 1. Create a table3 row with key 1
INSERT INTO table3 (c10, c6, c9, c3, c5) VALUES (1, 'BLUE', 0, 0, 'foo');

-- 2. Create two table1 rows, one with c6 = 'RED' and one with a different color
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c6, c2) VALUES (10, 'RED', 'something');
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c6, c2) VALUES (20, 'GREEN', 'another');

-- 3. Insert two table2 rows both referencing table3 key 1 but with different c4 values so that one joins to the red row and one to the non‐red row
INSERT INTO table2 (c8, c4, c10, c7, c1) VALUES (100, 10, 1, 'order1', 0);
INSERT INTO table2 (c8, c4, c10, c7, c1) VALUES (101, 20, 1, 'order2', 0);

-- Explanation of the effect:
-- For the table3 row with c10 = 1, Q1 (the correlated version) computes:
--    COUNT(*) = (number of table2 rows for which table2.c10 = 1 and the joined table1 row has c6 = 'RED')
-- In our data this count is 1 (because the first table2 row joins to the red table1 row), so Q1 will NOT return that row.
-- On the other hand, Q2’s uncorrelated subquery produces the set of all table3.c10 values for which there exists at least one red order. In our data the red order causes the subquery to include the value 1, and therefore Q2 excludes the table3 row with c10 = 1.

-- (If instead we removed the red order row from table2 then Q1 would return that table3 row while Q2 (being uncorrelated) would return it as well. Thus the two queries are not equivalent in the presence of conflicting order records for the same table3 key.)

