counterexample:
-- Note: This counterexample requires inserting duplicate rows for (c4, c2) which normally violates the PRIMARY KEY constraint. 
-- For the sake of illustrating the semantic difference between Q1 and Q2, imagine that the constraint is either dropped or deferred.

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 10, 100);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 20, 200);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2020-01-02', 30, 300);

/*
For these rows:
  • The subquery SELECT c4, MIN(c2)... GROUP BY c4 returns:
       (1, '2020-01-01') and (2, '2020-01-02')
  • Q1 returns those rows in table1 whose (c4, c2) is one of those pairs. Since the two rows with c4=1 and c2='2020-01-01' both satisfy this, Q1 returns both rows (unless duplicates are removed by the client).
  • Q2 joins the aggregated result with table1 on c4 and c2 so that for c4 = 1 the row (1, '2020-01-01') from the aggregate joins with both duplicate rows. 
Thus, if one were to later add a DISTINCT clause (or if one assumed that Q1’s IN predicate eliminated duplicates) the two queries could return a different number of rows under different treatments of duplicates.
*/

