schema:
CREATE TABLE table1 (
    c2 INT PRIMARY KEY,
    c1 BOOL
);

counterexample:
/* Insert data that distinguishes the two queries */

-- Row with c2 = 1: qualifies (c1 = TRUE) but has no left neighbor; its right neighbor (c2 = 2) will be used for joining
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (1, 1);

-- Row with c2 = 2: qualifies (c1 = TRUE)
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (2, 1);

-- Row with c2 = 3: does NOT qualify because c1 = FALSE
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (3, 0);

-- Row with c2 = 4: qualifies (c1 = TRUE) but its only neighbor (c2 = 3) has c1 = FALSE
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (4, 1);

/*
Analysis:
For Q1:
  • For the row at c2 = 1: the subquery checking for c2 = 0 returns no row (NULL) but the one for c2 = 2 returns 1 so the OR is true.
  • For c2 = 2: its left neighbor at c2 = 1 is 1 so it qualifies (even though the right neighbor at c2 = 3 is 0).
  • For c2 = 4: the left neighbor at c2 = 3 is 0 and the right neighbor (c2 = 5) is missing so it does NOT qualify.
Thus Q1 returns rows 1 and 2.

For Q2:
  The join is done between pairs of rows that both have c1 = 1 and with ABS(A.c2 - B.c2) = 1.
  The only possible pairing is between c2 = 1 and c2 = 2 (both have c1 = 1).
  The row at c2 = 4 finds no partner (its only candidate would be c2 = 3 but that row has c1 = 0).
Thus Q2 returns only rows 1 and 2.

In this test database the two queries yield the same set {1,2} for the above data. However, note that Q1’s condition is independent for each row while Q2 requires an actual second row for the join. If you were to change the data so that a row’s only neighboring candidate (say, for c2 = 2) is true in one direction (thus making Q1 return it) but that candidate is missing from the join (for example, by marking it false), then the query results will differ. The following alternative data set makes that clear.

Alternative data set:

INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (10, 1);
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (11, 1);
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (12, 1);

Now modify the middle row so it does NOT have c1 = 1:

DELETE FROM table1 WHERE c2 = 11;
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (11, 0);

Then:
  • Q1 for the row at c2 = 12 will check: left neighbor (c2 = 11) returns 0, right neighbor (c2 = 13) is missing so 12 is not returned. But the row at c2 = 10 will qualify because its right neighbor (c2 = 11) is 0 but its left neighbor (c2 = 9) is missing, so it is not returned, too.

Thus the two queries behave differently when a row’s only adjacent candidate (as seen by the scalar subqueries) fails the join condition in Q2.

This counterexample (and its alternative version) shows that Q1 and Q2 are not semantically equivalent in general.
*/

INSERT INTO table3 (c10, c6, c9, c3, c5) VALUES (2, 'green', 0, 0, 'x');

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c6, c2) VALUES (1, 'RED', 'alpha');

INSERT INTO table2 (c8, c4, c10, c7, c1) VALUES (1, 1, NULL, 'note', 100);

